    "hardest_part": "Inferring each group's hidden rules from visual examples and submission feedback, especially distinguishing competing explanations and solving EX-10 within the three-hour budget.",
    "likely_failure": "Spending too much time testing incorrect rule hypotheses or navigating prerequisites, leaving EX-10 or END unfinished. Misreading a symbol or accidentally submitting an active path could also waste turns.",
    "helpful_mechanics": "Single-player puzzles allow deliberate reasoning rather than fast reactions. Related panels within a group can provide evidence about shared rules. Saved progress and partial-panel scoring preserve incremental gains, and pointer movement can extend a path without holding a button.",
    "harmful_mechanics": "Rules must be inferred rather than supplied, and screenshot-only interaction can make subtle visual distinctions difficult. Each turn permits only one mouse input, so complex paths require multiple observation-action cycles. Clicking while a path is active submits it, making ordinary drag habits risky. Unlock dependencies and a wall-clock budget can prevent reaching later scoring milestones."
